// ============================================================================
// Hunt: FortiBleed campaign - FortiGate admin-account deletions and lockouts
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application), T1078 (Valid
//   Accounts), T1562.001 (Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Tools)
// Data: FortiGate syslog/event logs forwarded to your SIEM (Microsoft
//   Sentinel Syslog table shown as the example - point this at your
//   Fortinet syslog forwarder or the FortiGate connector table instead)
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-october-9-2026.html
// Logic: per an FBI + US Secret Service joint advisory (October 6, 2026),
// the FortiBleed credential-theft campaign is still active: 86,644+
// compromised Fortinet devices across 194 countries, with attackers deleting
// admin accounts and locking owners out of their own firewalls (BleepingComputer:
// "Ongoing FortiBleed attacks lock out FortiGate VPN admins"). Stolen VPN
// access is feeding INC/Lynx and Payload ransomware affiliates. Variant 1
// hunts the signature observable of this campaign - admin-account lifecycle
// changes (deletions and unexpected adds) in the firewall's own logs.
// Variant 2 hunts the precursor: credential-stuffing-style failure bursts
// against the admin GUI / SSL-VPN portal that precede a lockout.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: admin-account deletions and adds in FortiGate logs --
// FortiGate syslogs carry the admin action in the message. Legit admin
// changes are rare; a "delete admin" event from an external session is a
// lockout in progress. Baseline what normal admin activity looks like
// before alerting, then investigate every deletion and every add that
// did not come from your own change process.
Syslog
| where TimeGenerated > ago(14d)
| where SyslogMessage has "FortiGate" or ProcessName has "fortigate"
   or SyslogMessage has_any ("logid=", "devname=", "fortigate")
| where SyslogMessage has "admin"
| where SyslogMessage has_any ("delete", "deleted", "delet", "add ", "added", "creat")
   or SyslogMessage has_any ("admin deletion", "add admin", "new admin")
| extend AdminAction = case(
      SyslogMessage has "delete" or SyslogMessage has "delet", "admin-deleted",
      SyslogMessage has "add" or SyslogMessage has "creat", "admin-added",
      "admin-changed")
| summarize EventCount = count(),
            Sources = make_set(SyslogMessage, 3)
    by TimeGenerated = bin(TimeGenerated, 1h), HostName, AdminAction
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 2: admin GUI / SSL-VPN login failure bursts (the lockout precursor) --
// FortiBleed compromises start with harvested credentials; attackers then
// log in to finish the lockout. A burst of failed admin/VPN logins from a
// single external source - followed by any success - is the sequence this
// campaign's affiliates use. Thresholds are a starting point: baseline first.
Syslog
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where SyslogMessage has "FortiGate" or ProcessName has "fortigate"
   or SyslogMessage has_any ("logid=", "devname=", "fortigate")
| where SyslogMessage has_any ("login", "logon", "auth", "authenticate")
| extend LoginResult = case(
      SyslogMessage has_any ("fail", "denied", "error", "invalid"), "failed",
      SyslogMessage has_any ("success", "accepted", "permit"), "succeeded",
      "other")
| summarize FailedLogins = countif(LoginResult == "failed"),
            SuccessfulLogins = countif(LoginResult == "succeeded"),
            FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated), LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
    by bin(TimeGenerated, 1h), HostName
| order by FailedLogins desc, TimeGenerated desc
